---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const viteConfig = `import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import { mockApiPlugin } from '@wesflo/vite-plugin-local-mock-api';

export default defineConfig(({ command }) => ({
  plugins: command === 'serve' ? [
    mockApiPlugin({
      mockRoot: new URL('./mock/', import.meta.url),
      requestPrefixes: '/api/',
    }),
  ] : [],
}));`;

const fileTree = `mock/
├── PUT_profile.json
└── users/
    ├── profile.json
    └── POST_profile.json`;

const html = `<body>
  <your-micro-frontend></your-micro-frontend>
  <wf-vite-mock-proxy></wf-vite-mock-proxy>
  <script type="module" src="/src/index.ts"></script>
</body>`;

const entry = `import '@wesflo/wf-vite-mock-proxy';`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Getting started" description="Set up Lockxy in a Vite project." activePath="/getting-started/">
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Quick start</p>
        <h1>From zero to a local response.</h1>
        <p class="lead">Install the plugin and add a correctly named local file. That is the complete basic setup.</p>

        <h2 id="1-add-the-vite-plugin">1. Add the Vite plugin</h2>
        <Code code={viteConfig} label="vite.config.ts" />
        <Callout title="Development only">
            <p>
                The configuration above excludes the plugin from builds. As an additional safety net, the plugin refuses
                to register mock middleware in a production build or production mode and prints a prominent warning.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="2-add-a-mock-file">2. Add a mock file</h2>
        <Code code={fileTree} label="project structure" />
        <p>
            A <code>GET /api/users/profile</code> request resolves to <code>mock/users/profile.json</code>. For
            <code>POST</code>, the method-specific <code>POST_profile.json</code> wins before the generic file.
            With the shown files, the nested generic file also wins for <code>PUT</code>. Nesting is optional: once that
            more specific fallback is absent, <code>PUT /api/users/profile</code> resolves to the root-level
            <code>mock/PUT_profile.json</code>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="3-start-vite">3. Start Vite</h2>
        <Code code="pnpm dev" label="terminal" />
        <p>
            Send requests from your application as usual. The plugin answers matching requests inside Vite's development
            server, so no application-side fetch wrapper is required.
        </p>

        <Callout title="No manifest required">
            <p>
                The default file resolver is the primary workflow. Add <code>mock.manifest.json</code> only when selected
                calls need custom status codes, delays, failures, or multiple response variants.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="optional-mount-the-proxy-panel">Optional: mount the proxy panel</h2>
        <Code code={html} label="index.html" />
        <Code code={entry} label="src/index.ts" />
        <p>
            Install <code>@wesflo/wf-vite-mock-proxy</code> and mount it only if you want browser controls for scenarios or
            endpoint passthrough. The panel reads the optional manifest from the fixed
            <code>/_local-mock-api/manifest</code> route.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Panel stays optional">
            <p>
                The panel renders only after it loads a manifest containing configurable endpoints. Without a manifest—or
                with a convention-only or delay-only manifest—it adds no visible UI. A production deployment normally gets
                a 404 for the development-only manifest route and therefore renders nothing.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
